// About Viability Thinking (/about). The text and the three diagrams are taken word for word from
// mocks/about-viability-thinking.html (task sheet 2026-10-27); only the look is the Lab's. Do not edit the words here.
window.VTAbout = { html: "<header class=\"hero\">\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">Viability Thinking</p>\n    <h1>Will it work, and keep working, when it matters?</h1>\n    <p>A way of judging whether a service, an organisation or a change will deliver what it is for, keep delivering it as conditions change, and stay within what people can tolerate when it does not.</p>\n    <div class=\"contents\"><a href=\"#what\">What viability is</a><a href=\"#design\">Desirable, feasible, viable</a><a href=\"#testing\">Modelling and testing</a><a href=\"#leave\">What you leave with</a></div>\n  </header>\n\n  <section id=\"what\" class=\"sec\">\n    <div class=\"side\"><b>1</b>What viability is</div>\n    <div class=\"prose\">\n      <h2>A way of thinking</h2>\n      <p class=\"lead\">Each of the great management ways of thinking changed how leaders see one part of the problem. Thinking changed first, and practice followed.</p>\n      <div class=\"lenses five\" aria-label=\"Five ways of thinking\">\n        <div><b>Lean thinking</b>changed how leaders see the flow of value: what customers value, and the waste that gets in the way.</div>\n        <div><b>Systems thinking</b>changed how they see connections: feedback, interdependence and behaviour no single part explains.</div>\n        <div><b>Design thinking</b>changed how they see people's needs: framing problems from the side of those who use the service.</div>\n        <div><b>Resilience thinking</b>changed how they see disruption: as a normal part of the environment, to anticipate, absorb and adapt to.</div>\n        <div><b>Strategic thinking</b>changed how they see the future: the organisation's position in a changing environment, and the choices it must make to stay relevant.</div>\n        <div class=\"all\"><b>Viability Thinking</b>draws on all five and applies them to one question about one service: will it keep delivering what it is for, through what is changing around it, without causing harm that cannot be tolerated?</div>\n      </div>\n      <p>Viability Thinking is built in the same three layers: a philosophy (the principles), a methodology (the practices, from framing the problem to testing the changes), and a toolkit chosen for each piece of work.</p>\n\n      <h2 class=\"next\">What we mean by viability</h2>\n      <p class=\"lead\">Viability means able to live, or able to work. We use it for the services, organisations and initiatives our clients run and change.</p>\n      <p>A viable service will work, and keep working, in the conditions it will actually meet. It keeps its purpose and identity as its environment changes, and when something goes wrong it recovers and learns.</p>\n      <p>Most approaches look at one side only. Resilience work focuses on surviving the shock. Strategy and value work focus on growth, and operations work focuses on efficiency. Viability brings the three together.</p>\n      <div class=\"lenses\" aria-label=\"Three lenses on a hospital\">\n        <div><b>Strategy says</b>Treat more patients and win more contracts.</div>\n        <div><b>Operations says</b>Run every ward full and cut the time beds stand empty.</div>\n        <div><b>Resilience says</b>Keep beds free for winter, and cancel planned work when needed.</div>\n        <div class=\"all\"><b>Viability asks</b>How full can the wards run and still absorb a winter surge, without cancelling urgent operations?</div>\n      </div>\n      <p>Each lens is right, and each on its own causes harm. Followed alone, strategy fills the hospital beyond what it can carry, operations removes the spare beds that protected it, and resilience keeps patients waiting months for operations that could have gone ahead.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\">\n        <span class=\"tag\">The aim</span>\n        <p>An optimised (progressive) throughput that stays within tolerance (defensive), under severe but plausible scenarios, and can adapt to future demand.</p>\n        <p class=\"big\">Put more simply: more throughput, with no intolerable harm, and fit for the future.</p>\n      </div>\n      <figure>\n        <div class=\"frame\">\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 440\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"c1t c1d\">\n            <title id=\"c1t\">The viability curve</title>\n            <desc id=\"c1d\">Value delivered, net of harm, plotted against throughput. At zero throughput there is zero harm and zero value. Value rises as the service is run harder, peaks at the best balance with today's capacity, then falls as harm rises faster than value, until it crosses into intolerable harm. A dashed curve shows that adding capacity at the constraint moves the peak right and up.</desc>\n            <rect x=\"372\" y=\"24\" width=\"200\" height=\"370\" fill=\"var(--a-tone)\"/>\n            <text x=\"472\" y=\"46\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\">Productive range</text>\n            <rect x=\"684\" y=\"24\" width=\"56\" height=\"370\" fill=\"var(--a-accent-tint)\"/>\n            <text x=\"712\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--a-accent)\">Intolerable</text>\n            <text x=\"712\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--a-accent)\">harm</text>\n            <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"318\" x2=\"740\" y2=\"318\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n            <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"394\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n            <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"394\" x2=\"740\" y2=\"394\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n            <path d=\"M60 318 C 200 248, 300 200, 372 180 C 430 162, 470 150, 476 150 C 540 150, 600 190, 640 260 C 665 305, 685 330, 712 394\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"3.4\"/>\n            <path d=\"M260 220 C 340 180, 420 150, 480 135 C 510 128, 540 120, 555 120 C 620 120, 680 160, 740 238\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 6\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"318\" r=\"6\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"476\" cy=\"150\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"555\" cy=\"120\" r=\"6.5\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/>\n            <text x=\"350\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Best balance with</text>\n            <text x=\"350\" y=\"119\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">today's capacity</text>\n            <line x1=\"420\" y1=\"124\" x2=\"470\" y2=\"144\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n            <text x=\"590\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Add capacity at the</text>\n            <text x=\"590\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">constraint: the curve</text>\n            <text x=\"590\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">moves right and up</text>\n            <line x1=\"600\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"562\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink2)\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n            <text x=\"160\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Too cautious:</text>\n            <text x=\"160\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">harm is low, but</text>\n            <text x=\"160\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">value is given up</text>\n            <text x=\"548\" y=\"268\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Running fuller:</text>\n            <text x=\"548\" y=\"283\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">harm rises faster</text>\n            <text x=\"548\" y=\"298\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">than value</text>\n            <text x=\"72\" y=\"346\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Zero harm, zero value</text>\n            <text x=\"72\" y=\"361\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">(ban driving; stop the service)</text>\n            <text x=\"400\" y=\"424\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Throughput: how hard the service is run</text>\n            <text x=\"22\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\" transform=\"rotate(-90 22 200)\">Value delivered, net of harm</text>\n          </svg>\n        </div>\n        <figcaption>The viability curve. Run a service too cautiously and harm is low, but value is given up: banning driving ends road accidents and stops everyone travelling. Run it harder and value rises, up to the best balance with today's capacity. Beyond that, harm rises faster than value until it becomes intolerable. Adding capacity where the service is constrained moves the curve right and up, so more value can be delivered within tolerance.</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section id=\"design\" class=\"sec\">\n    <div class=\"side\"><b>2</b>The design thinking link</div>\n    <div class=\"prose\">\n      <h2>Desirable, feasible, viable, and within tolerance</h2>\n      <p class=\"lead\">Design thinking tests a new idea through three lenses. Is it desirable: do people want it? Is it feasible: can we deliver it? Is it viable: will it pay its way? A good idea sits where all three overlap.</p>\n      <p>We use the word viability more broadly. In our sense, a viable service is desirable, feasible and sustainable, and it stays that way under pressure, as demand changes, and without causing harm its users could not tolerate.</p>\n      <p>That adds two questions design thinking does not ask. First, does it stay within tolerance? An idea can be desirable, feasible and profitable, and still cause serious harm when it fails. Second, does it keep working over time and under stress? Design thinking judges an idea at the moment it is designed. We judge the running service and the change together, against severe but plausible scenarios, now and as demand changes.</p>\n      <figure>\n        <div class=\"frame\">\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 720 510\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"c2t c2d\">\n            <title id=\"c2t\">Desirable, feasible, viable, and within tolerance</title>\n            <desc id=\"c2d\">Three overlapping circles labelled desirable, feasible and pays its way, with their overlap marked as where design thinking stops. A wider boundary drawn around all three is labelled within tolerance, under severe but plausible scenarios, over time: viability as we use it.</desc>\n            <rect x=\"40\" y=\"18\" width=\"640\" height=\"484\" rx=\"230\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--a-accent)\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 7\"/>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"52\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.4\" fill=\"var(--a-accent)\">WITHIN TOLERANCE</text>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" fill=\"var(--a-accent)\">under severe but plausible scenarios, over time</text>\n            <circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"200\" r=\"118\" fill=\"var(--a-value)\" fill-opacity=\".10\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink2)\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"200\" r=\"118\" fill=\"var(--a-value)\" fill-opacity=\".10\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink2)\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"318\" r=\"118\" fill=\"var(--a-value)\" fill-opacity=\".10\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink2)\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/>\n            <text x=\"235\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\">Desirable</text>\n            <text x=\"235\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">people want it</text>\n            <text x=\"485\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\">Feasible</text>\n            <text x=\"485\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">we can deliver it</text>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"378\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\">Pays its way</text>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"397\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">design thinking's \"viable\"</text>\n            <circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"242\" r=\"5\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\"/>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"268\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\">where design</text>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"283\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\">thinking stops</text>\n            <text x=\"360\" y=\"466\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Viability as we use it: all of this, and it holds</text>\n          </svg>\n        </div>\n        <figcaption>The familiar three circles test whether an idea is worth pursuing. The boundary around them asks whether it will hold: within tolerance, under severe but plausible scenarios, and over time.</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section id=\"testing\" class=\"sec\">\n    <div class=\"side\"><b>3</b>Modelling and testing</div>\n    <div class=\"prose\">\n      <h2>Find out before it happens</h2>\n      <p class=\"lead drift\">No organisation should discover how it could drift into a crisis by having one.</p>\n      <div class=\"scenario\">\n        <p>An accident, disruption or other crisis occurs, and harm is done. An investigation makes recommendations, with the promise that once they are implemented, nothing like it will happen again.</p>\n        <p>Months later, on a Tuesday morning, another crisis unfolds. <span class=\"time\">10am</span>The press want an interview. <span class=\"time\">Lunchtime</span>The board, the regulator or a minister wants to know why the changes did not stop it.</p>\n        <p>The question for the executive team is whether the warning signs were already there, and why no one acted on them.</p>\n      </div>\n      <p>Most organisations manage this through a well-developed \"risk from\" framework: identify the threats, assess their likelihood and impact, set controls, score them and report. That framework is valuable and should be kept. But it answers only one question.</p>\n      <div class=\"two\">\n        <div class=\"from\"><b>Risk from</b><p>What could go wrong, and how do we stop it?</p></div>\n        <div class=\"of\"><b>Resilience of</b><p>What must continue to go right, even when things go wrong, and how do we know it will?</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <p>Most risk frameworks are strong on the first question and do not yet address the second. This is not a failure of risk management; it is a limit of what risk management is designed to do. A \"resilience of\" approach adds the outcomes that must continue whatever happens, the tolerance for disruption (how much harm, to how many people, for how long), the capacity to anticipate, absorb a shock and adapt, and testing that capacity instead of assuming it. Viability Thinking adds the value side too: where the service could deliver more, safely.</p>\n\n      <h3>How we model and test</h3>\n      <ol class=\"steps\">\n        <li><b>Agree the frame</b><span>What the service is, where it starts and ends, what it is for, who depends on it, and what must never happen.</span></li>\n        <li><b>Model how it works</b><span>What goes in, the steps, what comes out, what it relies on, and the decisions taken elsewhere that shape it.</span></li>\n        <li><b>Test it</b><span>Against severe but plausible scenarios built from your own risks and past events: in analysis, in a facilitated pre-mortem, or in a live exercise.</span></li>\n        <li><b>Find the changes</b><span>What would let it deliver more, absorb a shock and adapt, without crossing the threshold you have set.</span></li>\n      </ol>\n\n      <h3>What testing shows</h3>\n      <p>The chart below shows what this testing can reveal, for an illustrative service. Each circle is a possible change. Its position along the bottom shows how much more the service would deliver on a normal day. Its height shows the worst harm it would lead to across a set of severe but plausible scenarios, where 100 is the point at which harm becomes intolerable. Anything above the threshold line causes intolerable harm in at least one scenario.</p>\n<figure>\n        <div class=\"frame\">\n          <svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 440\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"c3t c3d\">\n            <title id=\"c3t\">Testing possible changes</title>\n            <desc id=\"c3d\">An illustrative service. Each circle is a possible change, placed by the gain in throughput it gives on a normal day and by the worst harm it leads to across severe but plausible scenarios, as a share of the intolerable threshold. Running fuller and adding capacity at the constraint both cross the threshold. A back-up team lowers harm but adds no throughput. Only adding capacity at both constraints with a back-up team that notices quickly gives more throughput and stays within tolerance.</desc>\n            <rect x=\"80\" y=\"30\" width=\"660\" height=\"100.0\" fill=\"var(--a-accent-tint)\"/>\n            <text x=\"92\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--a-accent)\">Intolerable harm in at least one scenario</text>\n            <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"130.0\" x2=\"740\" y2=\"130.0\" stroke=\"var(--a-accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n            <text x=\"92\" y=\"150.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">Within tolerance in every scenario tested</text>\n            <line x1=\"371.8\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"371.8\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"var(--a-rule)\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n            <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"740\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n            <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n            <line x1=\"80.0\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"80.0\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">-40%</text><line x1=\"225.9\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"225.9\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\"/><text x=\"225.9\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">-20%</text><line x1=\"371.8\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"371.8\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\"/><text x=\"371.8\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">0%</text><line x1=\"517.6\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"517.6\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\"/><text x=\"517.6\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">+20%</text><line x1=\"663.5\" y1=\"380\" x2=\"663.5\" y2=\"385\" stroke=\"var(--a-faint)\"/><text x=\"663.5\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">+40%</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"384.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">0</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"259.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">50</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"134.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">100</text>\n            <line x1=\"377.8\" y1=\"150.0\" x2=\"465.9\" y2=\"96.0\" stroke=\"var(--a-muted)\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 5\"/>\n            <circle cx=\"371.8\" cy=\"155.0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"357.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" y=\"159.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Today</text>\n            <text x=\"383.8\" y=\"177.0\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">also: capacity where it is not the constraint</text>\n            <circle cx=\"145.6\" cy=\"300.0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"157.6\" y=\"304.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Restrict the service</text>\n            <circle cx=\"473.9\" cy=\"90.0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"461.9\" text-anchor=\"end\" y=\"94.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Run fuller</text>\n            <circle cx=\"524.9\" cy=\"80.0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"536.9\" y=\"84.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Add capacity at the constraint</text><text x=\"536.9\" y=\"99.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">(with or without a back-up team)</text>\n            <circle cx=\"371.8\" cy=\"230.0\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-raised)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"357.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" y=\"234.0\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">A back-up team</text>\n            <circle cx=\"524.9\" cy=\"192.5\" r=\"7\" fill=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke=\"var(--a-ink)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"536.9\" y=\"196.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\" font-weight=\"700\">Capacity at both constraints,</text><text x=\"536.9\" y=\"211.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">with a back-up team that</text><text x=\"536.9\" y=\"226.5\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">notices quickly</text>\n            <text x=\"90\" y=\"372\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--a-muted)\">Stop the service: no throughput, no harm (off the chart)</text>\n            <text x=\"410\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\">Gain in throughput on a normal day</text>\n            <text x=\"22\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"var(--a-ink2)\" transform=\"rotate(-90 22 205)\">Worst harm in the scenarios (100 = intolerable)</text>\n          </svg>\n        </div>\n        <figcaption>An illustrative service, tested against several severe but plausible scenarios. Each circle is one possible change.</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n      <p>Reading the chart from today's position:</p>\n      <ul class=\"leave\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1fr\">\n        <li><b>Run fuller.</b> The service delivers about 14 per cent more on a normal day, but in at least one scenario the harm becomes intolerable. This is the change most organisations make first, because the gain is visible and the cost is not.</li>\n        <li><b>Restrict or stop the service.</b> Harm falls, but so does throughput. Stopping it altogether removes the harm and the value with it.</li>\n        <li><b>Add capacity where it is not the constraint.</b> Nothing changes. The money is spent in the wrong place.</li>\n        <li><b>Add capacity at the constraint.</b> Throughput rises by about 21 per cent, but harm rises too, because the strain moves to the next weakest point. A back-up team on its own does not prevent this.</li>\n        <li><b>A back-up team.</b> Harm falls well below the threshold line, but the service delivers no more.</li>\n        <li><b>Capacity at both constraints, with a back-up team that notices quickly.</b> This is the only change that gives more throughput and stays within tolerance in every scenario tested.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p style=\"margin-top:22px\">The point is not the particular answer, which depends on the service. It is that changes which look sensible on a normal day can behave very differently under strain, and that the combination which works is often not the first one anyone would choose. Testing shows this before the change is made, not after.</p>\n      <p>The live exercise is the most demanding test. Over two days, your executive team experiences a crisis built from your own risks and conditions, manages it as it unfolds, faces the press, and then works out what it would need to change. It is confidential, and it shows the team how it could drift into a crisis without having to have one.</p>\n\n      <h3>For regulators</h3>\n      <p>Regulators face a double question: is the sector resilient enough, and are we? Regulating from a resilience perspective means judging how well a regulated organisation will perform and recover under strain, not only whether its risk analysis is rigorous and its controls reasonable. The same judgement applies to the regulator itself: where its outcomes depend on a few people or processes, where its systems hold or break under pressure, and how its decisions are made when it matters.</p>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section id=\"leave\" class=\"sec\">\n    <div class=\"side\"><b>4</b>What you leave with</div>\n    <div class=\"prose\">\n      <h2>What you leave with</h2>\n      <ul class=\"leave\">\n        <li>A tested view of where your service, and the system around it, could become more resilient and deliver more.</li>\n        <li>Leadership rehearsed under pressure, including facing the press.</li>\n        <li>A repeatable method for testing your most important risks before they happen.</li>\n        <li>A short report for your executive team and your audit and risk committee: the main vulnerabilities, the proposed changes, who owns each follow-up action, and recommendations.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p style=\"margin-top:28px;color:var(--a-ink2)\">Our work with regulators includes the Office of Rail and Road, the Financial Conduct Authority, the Care Quality Commission and the Scottish Housing Regulator, and with organisations across transport, financial services, healthcare, housing, energy, civil aviation and defence.</p>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <div class=\"close\" id=\"startOne\">\n    <h2>Start with one service</h2>\n    <p>Most clients begin with a single service that matters: one they rely on, or one that is about to change. We agree the frame with you, then model and test it together.</p>\n    <a class=\"btn\" id=\"aboutStart\" href=\"/hub\">Start here</a>\n  </div>\n  " };
